 * Emitted as static/npdev-expr.js (BusinessUiEmitter), loaded by the aggregate workbench page.
 *
 * F6-5: the browser twin of the platform's server-side expression engine
 * (NPDevContract/dsl .../expr/ComputedExpression.java). Same grammar, same coercion, same null
 * semantics, so an expression that validates and evaluates on the server evaluates to the same value
 * here with no server round trip. The two are pinned to ONE shared fixture
 * (NPDevContract/dsl/src/test/resources/expr-parity.json): the Java test and
 * scripts/quality/check-client-expr-parity.py both replay it, so they cannot drift silently.
 *
 * Language: number / string ('..' or "..", no escapes) / true / false / null literals, field references
 * (dotted paths, an optional leading $ such as $root.discount), arithmetic + - * / %, comparison
 * == != < <= > >=, logical && || !, unary -, parentheses, and function calls name(args) /
 * receiver.name(args) (a function must be supplied by the caller; none are built in, exactly like the
 * server's computed columns), with param => body lambdas as call arguments.
 *
 * Known, deliberate divergences from the JVM: a number prints with JavaScript's Number formatting
 * (1e21 is "1e+21", not "1.0E21"), and a string is "numeric" by a JavaScript re-implementation of
 * Double.parseDouble's grammar (decimal and exponent forms, NaN, Infinity, a trailing d/f/D/F; not hex floats).
